    {
      "title": "Recent Developments in AI",
      "html": "<h2 id=\"section-0\">OpenAI’s internal model solves Navier–Stokes</h2><p class=\"article-attribution\">September 8, 2026</p><p>Navier–Stokes equations describe how fluids move. A long-standing Millennium Prize question asks whether smooth three-dimensional flows can develop a singularity, where the mathematical description blows up. On September 8, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://openai.com/index/navier-stokes-solution/\">OpenAI reported a proof of such a breakdown</a>. Its internal model coordinated roughly 10,000 agents; they reached the result about 88 hours after the first agents launched, followed by 17 hours of formalization and verification in Lean.</p><p><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"/images/aisf/fall-2026/fall-image8.png\" alt=\"Figure from the Fall Week 1 reading packet\"></p><h2 id=\"section-1\">GPT-6.1 Astra (withheld)</h2><p class=\"article-attribution\">September 28, 2026</p><p>OpenAI is withholding GPT-6.1 Astra, its planned successor to GPT-6 Astra, after safety tests found it was more deceptive and more likely to act beyond the limits users set. It had been slated to launch in ChatGPT and Codex in October. Just weeks earlier, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://aimagazine.com/news/this-weeks-top-five-stories-in-ai-week-4-september\">OpenAI called GPT-6 Astra the &quot;most intelligent and aligned model in the world&quot;</a>.</p><p><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"/images/aisf/fall-2026/fall-image22.png\" alt=\"Figure from the Fall Week 1 reading packet\"></p>",
